(repin-citations.vox - keep every LANGUAGE.md line citation valid when
 the manual changes.

 The problem in one sentence: a citation names a LINE NUMBER, and every
 Vox release inserts sentences into LANGUAGE.md, so every citation below
 an insertion silently starts pointing at the wrong claim. There are
 about 1400 of them in this repo - the 26 claim ledgers' `line` column,
 the probes' header comments, the ledgers' `Source:` ranges, generator
 comments in src/ and tests/ - and nobody is going to re-check them by
 hand after each release. `scripts/check-citations.sh` catches only the
 citations that land on a blank line, a code fence or a bare heading; a
 shift onto adjacent prose is invisible to it, and adjacent prose is
 where a shifted citation nearly always lands.

 What this does instead: `diff -U0` the PINNED manual against the new
 one, turn the hunks into a function old line -> new line, and rewrite
 every citation through it. Lines above the first hunk keep their number;
 lines below a hunk shift by the running (added minus removed); lines
 INSIDE a hunk's removed range have no successor by arithmetic, so they
 are looked for by their own text within 40 lines of where they should
 be. A claim that merely moved is recovered and reported `moved`; only a
 claim whose text really changed becomes `?`, and is listed for a human.
 That is the whole design: arithmetic where arithmetic is sound, text
 search where it is not, and a visible `?` where neither works, rather
 than a plausible-looking wrong number.

 Why `?` is written into the file rather than the old number left alone:
 a citation whose claim was edited is not "probably still fine". Someone
 has to read the manual and the row together, and the only reliable way
 to make that happen is to make the file say so. The run exits non-zero
 when any `?` was produced, so a master or a CI job notices.

 Why in Vox: the same reason scripts/invariants.vox is (README, "Why
 it's written in Vox") - a systems tool in a young language finds bugs
 by existing. Nothing here needed a construct the language lacks. The
 two things Vox cannot do alone are run `diff` and list a directory, and
 both go through the same fork/Execute/reap `run shell` that
 invariants.vox and src/harness.vox already use.

 Usage:
   scripts/repin-citations [--to <path|commit>] [--from <path|commit>]
                           [--dry-run | --apply] [--pin]
                           [--scan <dir>] [--vox-repo <dir>] [--verbose]

   --to      the NEW manual: a path, or a commit in the vox repo.
             Default: the vox repo's working-tree LANGUAGE.md.
   --from    the OLD manual, overriding docs/ledger/PINNED-MANUAL.
   --dry-run print the table, change nothing. The default.
   --apply   rewrite the citations in place.
   --pin     with --apply: re-point PINNED-MANUAL, refresh
             docs/ledger/pinned/LANGUAGE.md, update INDEX.md's header.
   --scan    scan this directory tree instead of the repo's normal set
             (the self-test uses it; nothing else should need it).
   --verbose list every citation, not only the ones that moved.

 Paths are resolved from the repo root; the scripts/repin-citations
 wrapper makes any relative --to/--from/--scan absolute and then cds
 there, so both spellings work from anywhere.

 Exit codes: 0 clean, 1 at least one citation could not be mapped, 2 a
 usage or I/O error.)

(---------------------------------------------------------------------
 configuration and state

 Every global lives here, above the functions that read it, because a
 function may only reach a name already declared - and because a tool
 whose whole job is rewriting other people's files should keep its state
 in one readable place.
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------)

a text called 'the vox repo' is "../vox".
a text called 'the pin path' is "docs/ledger/PINNED-MANUAL".
a text called 'the pinned copy path' is "docs/ledger/pinned/LANGUAGE.md".
a text called 'the index path' is "docs/ledger/INDEX.md".

(How far either side of the arithmetic position a changed line's text is
 looked for. 40 is generous enough to survive a whole section being
 rewritten above the citation, and tight enough that a one-line claim
 like "Positions are 1-indexed" cannot match some unrelated repetition of
 itself elsewhere in a 5000-line manual.)
a number called 'the recovery window' is 40.

(command line)
a text called 'the to argument' is "".
a text called 'the from argument' is "".
a text called 'the scan directory' is "".
a boolean called 'applying changes' is false.
a boolean called 'pinning' is false.
a boolean called 'listing everything' is false.

(the two manuals, as lists of lines)
a list called 'the old lines' is [].
a list called 'the new lines' is [].
a text called 'the new manual path' is "".
a text called 'the scratch directory' is "./vf_repin".
a boolean called 'the vox repo is present' is false.

(what PINNED-MANUAL currently says)
a text called 'the pinned commit' is "".
a number called 'the pinned line count' is 0.

(one entry per diff hunk, in old-file order)
a list called 'hunk old start' is [].
a list called 'hunk old count' is [].
a list called 'hunk new count' is [].
a boolean called 'the map is identity' is true.

(how the last 'map old line' call answered: 0 unmoved, 1 shifted by
 arithmetic, 2 recovered by text search, 3 no answer)
a number called 'the map verdict' is 0.

(scratch shared by the scanners: 'the line bytes' is the line being read,
 'the rewritten line' the line being built, 'the file output' the whole
 file being built.)
a buffer called 'the line bytes'.
a buffer called 'the rewritten line'.
a buffer called 'the file output'.
a buffer called 'one output byte' is " ".
a boolean called 'the line changed' is false.
a text called 'the current label' is "".
a boolean called 'the file ended with a newline' is false.
(A Vox text ends at the first NUL, so a file holding one would come back
 from 'lines of' silently truncated - and a tool that then WROTE that
 back would delete the rest of the file. Nothing in docs/, src/ or
 tests/ holds a NUL today; this is here so that if one ever does, the
 run stops instead of eating it.)
a boolean called 'a nul byte was seen' is false.

(the citation cell of the ledger table row being read; 0 when the row has
 none)
a number called 'the cell start' is 0.
a number called 'the cell stop' is 0.

(set by 'number at' to the position just past the digits it read, and by
 'rewrite run' to the position just past the citation it consumed. Vox
 returns one value; these are the second.)
a number called 'the digits end' is 0.
a number called 'the run end' is 0.

(the report)
a number called 'citations checked' is 0.
a number called 'citations shifted' is 0.
a number called 'citations moved' is 0.
a number called 'citations broken' is 0.
a number called 'cells for review' is 0.
a number called 'files scanned' is 0.
a number called 'files rewritten' is 0.

(---------------------------------------------------------------------
 shell and file helpers - the same shape scripts/invariants.vox uses,
 kept local so this tool depends on nothing in src/.
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------)

To 'run shell' with a text called 'the command'.
    a list called 'the shell arguments' is [].
    append "-c" to 'the shell arguments'.
    append 'the command' to 'the shell arguments'.
    a number called 'the child' is fork the process.
    If 'the child' is 0 then, Execute "/bin/sh" with arguments 'the shell arguments', Exit 127.
    a number called 'the reaped' is reap process 'the child'.
    (`reap process` yields the PID; the wait status is a separate
     expression - LANGUAGE.md, "The reaped status".)
    a number called 'the status' is the reaped status.
    a number called 'the terminating signal' is 'the status' bit-and 127.
    If 'the terminating signal' is not 0 then, Return a number, 128 add 'the terminating signal'.
    Return a number, 'the status' divide 256 modulo 256.

(single-quote a value POSIX-style, so a path holding spaces or quotes
 cannot break a command line)
To 'shell quote' with a text called 'the value'.
    a buffer called 'the source bytes' is 'the value'.
    a buffer called 'the quoted bytes' is "'".
    a number called 'the scan position' is 1.
    a number called 'the byte count' is 'the source bytes's size.
    a buffer called 'one byte' is " ".
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the byte count',
        a number called 'the current byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'the source bytes',
        If 'the current byte' is 39 then, append "'\\''" to 'the quoted bytes'.
        If 'the current byte' is not 39 then, Set byte 1 of 'one byte' to 'the current byte', append 'one byte' to 'the quoted bytes'.
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    append "'" to 'the quoted bytes'.
    a text called 'the result' is 'the quoted bytes' as text.
    Return a text, 'the result'.

To 'path exists' with a text called 'the path'.
    a number called 'the code' is 'run shell' of "test -e {'shell quote' of 'the path'}".
    Return a boolean, 'the code' is 0.

(A file to a list of its lines, newline stripped, leaving 'the file ended
 with a newline' behind for a caller that will write the file back. Not a
 `Read line` loop: that cannot tell a blank line from end of file without
 a separate check, and LANGUAGE.md is a third blank lines. One Read fills
 a dynamic buffer with the whole file and the split happens here, which
 also makes the trailing-newline question answerable - and that one byte
 is the difference between a rewritten file that is byte-identical
 outside its citations and one that is not.)
To 'lines of' with a text called 'the path'.
    a list called 'the result' is [].
    open a file for reading called 'the input file' at 'the path'.
    On error Print "repin-citations: cannot read {'the path'}", Exit 2.
    a buffer called 'the file bytes'.
    Read from 'the input file' into 'the file bytes'.
    Close 'the input file'.
    a number called 'the byte count' is 'the file bytes's size.
    Set 'the file ended with a newline' to false.
    Set 'a nul byte was seen' to false.
    a number called 'the final byte' is 0.
    If 'the byte count' is greater than 0 then, Set 'the final byte' to byte 'the byte count' of 'the file bytes'.
    If 'the final byte' is 10 then, Set 'the file ended with a newline' to true.
    a buffer called 'the current line'.
    a buffer called 'one byte' is " ".
    a number called 'the scan position' is 1.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the byte count',
        a number called 'the current byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'the file bytes',
        If 'the current byte' is 10 then,
            a buffer called 'the line copy' is 'the current line',
            a text called 'the finished line' is 'the line copy' as text,
            append 'the finished line' to 'the result',
            clear 'the current line'.
        If 'the current byte' is not 10 then,
            Set byte 1 of 'one byte' to 'the current byte',
            append 'one byte' to 'the current line'.
        If 'the current byte' is 0 then, Set 'a nul byte was seen' to true.
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    If 'a nul byte was seen' then,
        Print "repin-citations: {'the path'} holds a NUL byte, which a Vox text ends at - refusing to read it rather than truncating it",
        Exit 2.
    If 'the current line's size is greater than 0 then,
        a buffer called 'the tail copy' is 'the current line',
        a text called 'the tail line' is 'the tail copy' as text,
        append 'the tail line' to 'the result'.
    Return a list, 'the result'.

(Write whatever 'the file output' holds to a path. A buffer, never a
 format string: a rewritten ledger line is full of `{`, and a format
 string would read those as interpolations.)
To 'write the output to' with a text called 'the path'.
    open a file for writing called 'the output file' at 'the path'.
    On error Print "repin-citations: cannot write {'the path'}", Exit 2.
    Write 'the file output' to 'the output file'.
    On error Print "repin-citations: write failed on {'the path'}", Exit 2.
    Close 'the output file'.

(Vox's `append` wants a buffer on both sides, so a text goes through a
 buffer declared from it. These two are where that happens, rather than
 three copies of the same three lines.)
To 'load the line' with a text called 'the value'.
    clear 'the line bytes'.
    a buffer called 'the value bytes' is 'the value'.
    append 'the value bytes' to 'the line bytes'.

To 'append text to the output' with a text called 'the value'.
    a buffer called 'the value bytes' is 'the value'.
    append 'the value bytes' to 'the file output'.

(The first line of a file, or "" - how a one-line shell result (a hash, a
 date) is read back, since Vox cannot capture a child's stdout directly.)
To 'first line of' with a text called 'the path'.
    a list called 'the read lines' is 'lines of' of 'the path'.
    If 'the read lines's length is 0 then, Return a text, "".
    a text called 'the first' is element 1 of 'the read lines'.
    Return a text, 'the first'.

(---------------------------------------------------------------------
 byte-level scanning primitives. Everything under the diff works on a
 buffer and a 1-based position, because that is the shape Vox gives for
 looking inside text.
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------)

To 'matches at' with a buffer called 'the haystack' and a number called 'the position' and a text called 'the needle'.
    a buffer called 'the needle bytes' is 'the needle'.
    a number called 'the needle length' is 'the needle bytes's size.
    a number called 'the haystack length' is 'the haystack's size.
    If 'the position' is less than 1 then, Return a boolean, false.
    a number called 'the last needed' is {'the position' add 'the needle length'} subtract 1.
    If 'the last needed' is greater than 'the haystack length' then, Return a boolean, false.
    a boolean called 'the answer' is true.
    a number called 'the offset' is 0.
    While 'the offset' is less than 'the needle length' and 'the answer',
        a number called 'the haystack byte' is byte {'the position' add 'the offset'} of 'the haystack',
        a number called 'the needle byte' is byte {'the offset' add 1} of 'the needle bytes',
        If 'the haystack byte' is not 'the needle byte' then, Set 'the answer' to false.
        Set 'the offset' to 'the offset' add 1.
    Return a boolean, 'the answer'.

To 'digit at' with a buffer called 'the haystack' and a number called 'the position'.
    If 'the position' is less than 1 then, Return a boolean, false.
    If 'the position' is greater than 'the haystack's size then, Return a boolean, false.
    a number called 'the current byte' is byte 'the position' of 'the haystack'.
    If 'the current byte' is less than 48 then, Return a boolean, false.
    If 'the current byte' is greater than 57 then, Return a boolean, false.
    Return a boolean, true.

(A dash joining the two ends of a range. Three spellings are in the
 corpus - the ASCII hyphen, and the en dash the ledgers mostly use, which
 is three bytes of UTF-8 - and a rewritten range keeps whichever one it
 found rather than normalising, because normalising would touch citations
 the manual never moved.)
To 'dash length at' with a buffer called 'the haystack' and a number called 'the position'.
    a number called 'the length' is 0.
    a boolean called 'an ascii hyphen' is 'matches at' of 'the haystack' and 'the position' and "-".
    If 'an ascii hyphen' then, Set 'the length' to 1.
    a boolean called 'an en dash' is 'matches at' of 'the haystack' and 'the position' and "–".
    If 'an en dash' then, Set 'the length' to 3.
    a boolean called 'an em dash' is 'matches at' of 'the haystack' and 'the position' and "—".
    If 'an em dash' then, Set 'the length' to 3.
    Return a number, 'the length'.

To 'number at' with a buffer called 'the haystack' and a number called 'the position'.
    a number called 'the value' is 0.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 'the position'.
    a number called 'the haystack length' is 'the haystack's size.
    a boolean called 'still in digits' is true.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the haystack length' and 'still in digits',
        a number called 'the current byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'the haystack',
        If 'the current byte' is less than 48 then, Set 'still in digits' to false.
        If 'the current byte' is greater than 57 then, Set 'still in digits' to false.
        If 'still in digits' then,
            Set 'the value' to {'the value' multiply 10} add {'the current byte' subtract 48},
            Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1..
    Set 'the digits end' to 'the scan position'.
    Return a number, 'the value'.

(Does any byte in [start, stop] look like a digit? This is how a ledger
 `line` cell holding a number this tool did not rewrite - a probe
 filename, a version string, a line number buried in prose - reaches a
 human instead of quietly going stale.)
To 'any digit between' with a buffer called 'the haystack' and a number called 'the start' and a number called 'the stop'.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 'the start'.
    a boolean called 'the answer' is false.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the stop',
        a boolean called 'a digit here' is 'digit at' of 'the haystack' and 'the scan position',
        If 'a digit here' then, Set 'the answer' to true.
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    Return a boolean, 'the answer'.

(The rest of a line from a position, as text. PINNED-MANUAL's fields are
 key, space, value, so the value is whatever follows the key.)
To 'rest of line from' with a buffer called 'the haystack' and a number called 'the position'.
    a buffer called 'the tail bytes'.
    a buffer called 'one byte' is " ".
    a number called 'the haystack length' is 'the haystack's size.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 'the position'.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the haystack length',
        a number called 'the current byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'the haystack',
        Set byte 1 of 'one byte' to 'the current byte',
        append 'one byte' to 'the tail bytes',
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    a buffer called 'the tail copy' is 'the tail bytes'.
    a text called 'the tail text' is 'the tail copy' as text.
    Return a text, 'the tail text'.

(How many decimal digits a number is written with - needed to step over a
 number that has just been replaced by one of a different width.)
To 'digit width of' with a number called 'the value'.
    a number called 'the width' is 1.
    a number called 'the remainder' is 'the value' divide 10.
    While 'the remainder' is greater than 0,
        Set 'the width' to 'the width' add 1,
        Set 'the remainder' to 'the remainder' divide 10.
    Return a number, 'the width'.

(---------------------------------------------------------------------
 the line map
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------)

(`@@ -<start>[,<count>] +<start>[,<count>] @@`. A missing count means 1;
 a count of 0 on the old side means the hunk inserts after that line and
 removes nothing, which is the case the arithmetic below turns on.)
To 'parse hunk header' with a text called 'the header line'.
    a buffer called 'the header bytes' is 'the header line'.
    a boolean called 'a hunk header' is 'matches at' of 'the header bytes' and 1 and "@@ -".
    If 'a hunk header' is false then, Return a boolean, false.
    a number called 'the old start' is 'number at' of 'the header bytes' and 5.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 'the digits end'.
    a number called 'the removed' is 1.
    a boolean called 'a comma here' is 'matches at' of 'the header bytes' and 'the scan position' and ",".
    If 'a comma here' then,
        Set 'the removed' to 'number at' of 'the header bytes' and {'the scan position' add 1},
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the digits end'.
    a boolean called 'a plus here' is 'matches at' of 'the header bytes' and 'the scan position' and " +".
    If 'a plus here' is false then, Return a boolean, false.
    a number called 'the new start' is 'number at' of 'the header bytes' and {'the scan position' add 2}.
    Set 'the scan position' to 'the digits end'.
    a number called 'the added' is 1.
    a boolean called 'a second comma' is 'matches at' of 'the header bytes' and 'the scan position' and ",".
    If 'a second comma' then, Set 'the added' to 'number at' of 'the header bytes' and {'the scan position' add 1}.
    append 'the old start' to 'hunk old start'.
    append 'the removed' to 'hunk old count'.
    append 'the added' to 'hunk new count'.
    Return a boolean, true.

To 'read hunks from' with a text called 'the diff path'.
    Set 'hunk old start' to [].
    Set 'hunk old count' to [].
    Set 'hunk new count' to [].
    a list called 'the diff lines' is 'lines of' of 'the diff path'.
    a number called 'the diff line count' is 'the diff lines's length.
    a number called 'the index' is 1.
    While 'the index' is less than or equal to 'the diff line count',
        a text called 'the diff line' is element 'the index' of 'the diff lines',
        a boolean called 'was a header' is 'parse hunk header' of 'the diff line',
        Set 'the index' to 'the index' add 1.

(A line with its trailing blanks removed. Recovery compares lines this
 way: a doc-hygiene pass that strips trailing whitespace rewrites hundreds
 of manual lines without touching a single claim, and a citation into one
 of them has not gone stale - it would be dishonest to mark it `?`.
 Leading whitespace is NOT trimmed, because indentation inside a fenced
 example is part of the claim.)
To 'trailing trimmed' with a text called 'the value'.
    a buffer called 'the value bytes' is 'the value'.
    a number called 'the stop' is 'the value bytes's size.
    a boolean called 'still trimming' is true.
    While 'still trimming' and 'the stop' is greater than 0,
        a number called 'the current byte' is byte 'the stop' of 'the value bytes',
        a boolean called 'a blank byte' is false,
        If 'the current byte' is 32 then, Set 'a blank byte' to true.
        If 'the current byte' is 9 then, Set 'a blank byte' to true.
        If 'the current byte' is 13 then, Set 'a blank byte' to true.
        If 'a blank byte' then, Set 'the stop' to 'the stop' subtract 1.
        If 'a blank byte' is false then, Set 'still trimming' to false..
    a buffer called 'the trimmed bytes'.
    a buffer called 'one byte' is " ".
    a number called 'the scan position' is 1.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the stop',
        a number called 'the copied byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'the value bytes',
        Set byte 1 of 'one byte' to 'the copied byte',
        append 'one byte' to 'the trimmed bytes',
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    a buffer called 'the trimmed copy' is 'the trimmed bytes'.
    a text called 'the trimmed text' is 'the trimmed copy' as text.
    Return a text, 'the trimmed text'.

(A line the diff calls removed or replaced has no successor by
 arithmetic. Look for its exact text near where it ought to be: found
 exactly once, that IS the claim, moved. Found never, or found more than
 once, and the tool refuses to guess - which is what keeps this safe on a
 blank line or a `---` rule, whose text matches so often it can never
 match exactly once.)
To 'recover line' with a number called 'the old line' and a number called 'the expected'.
    a text called 'the raw wanted' is element 'the old line' of 'the old lines'.
    a text called 'the wanted text' is 'trailing trimmed' of 'the raw wanted'.
    a number called 'the new line count' is 'the new lines's length.
    a number called 'the window start' is 'the expected' subtract 'the recovery window'.
    If 'the window start' is less than 1 then, Set 'the window start' to 1.
    a number called 'the window stop' is 'the expected' add 'the recovery window'.
    If 'the window stop' is greater than 'the new line count' then, Set 'the window stop' to 'the new line count'.
    a number called 'the hits' is 0.
    a number called 'the hit line' is 0.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 'the window start'.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the window stop',
        a text called 'the raw candidate' is element 'the scan position' of 'the new lines',
        a text called 'the candidate' is 'trailing trimmed' of 'the raw candidate',
        If 'the candidate' is 'the wanted text' then,
            Set 'the hits' to 'the hits' add 1,
            Set 'the hit line' to 'the scan position'.
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    If 'the hits' is 1 then, Return a number, 'the hit line'.
    Return a number, 0.

To 'map old line' with a number called 'the old line'.
    Set 'the map verdict' to 3.
    If 'the old line' is less than 1 then, Return a number, 0.
    If 'the old line' is greater than 'the old lines's length then, Return a number, 0.
    a number called 'the delta' is 0.
    a boolean called 'inside a hunk' is false.
    a boolean called 'still scanning' is true.
    a number called 'the hunk count' is 'hunk old start's length.
    a number called 'the index' is 1.
    While 'the index' is less than or equal to 'the hunk count' and 'still scanning',
        a number called 'the start' is element 'the index' of 'hunk old start',
        a number called 'the removed' is element 'the index' of 'hunk old count',
        a number called 'the added' is element 'the index' of 'hunk new count',
        a number called 'the stop' is 0,
        If 'the removed' is 0 then,
            If 'the old line' is greater than 'the start' then, Set 'the delta' to 'the delta' add 'the added'.
            If 'the old line' is less than or equal to 'the start' then, Set 'still scanning' to false..
        If 'the removed' is greater than 0 then,
            Set 'the stop' to {'the start' add 'the removed'} subtract 1,
            If 'the old line' is greater than 'the stop' then, Set 'the delta' to 'the delta' add {'the added' subtract 'the removed'}.
            If 'the old line' is less than 'the start' then, Set 'still scanning' to false.
            If 'the old line' is greater than or equal to 'the start' and 'the old line' is less than or equal to 'the stop' then, Set 'inside a hunk' to true, Set 'still scanning' to false..
        Set 'the index' to 'the index' add 1.
    a number called 'the expected' is 'the old line' add 'the delta'.
    If 'inside a hunk' is false then,
        Set 'the map verdict' to 0,
        If 'the delta' is not 0 then, Set 'the map verdict' to 1.
        Return a number, 'the expected'.
    a number called 'the recovered' is 'recover line' of 'the old line' and 'the expected'.
    (A line recovered at the number it already had did not move; saying
     `moved` there would rewrite nothing and report a change that is not
     one, which is how a whitespace-only manual pass would otherwise show
     up as hundreds of edits.)
    If 'the recovered' is 'the old line' then,
        Set 'the map verdict' to 0,
        Return a number, 'the recovered'.
    If 'the recovered' is greater than 0 then,
        Set 'the map verdict' to 2,
        Return a number, 'the recovered'.
    Set 'the map verdict' to 3.
    Return a number, 0.

(---------------------------------------------------------------------
 rewriting one citation
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------)

To 'copy slice' with a number called 'the start' and a number called 'the stop'.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 'the start'.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the stop',
        a number called 'the current byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'the line bytes',
        Set byte 1 of 'one output byte' to 'the current byte',
        append 'one output byte' to 'the rewritten line',
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.

To 'report citation' with a number called 'the old line' and a text called 'the new text' and a text called 'the verdict'.
    Print "{'the current label'}  {'the old line'} -> {'the new text'}  {'the verdict'}".

(One number of a citation: map it, append the replacement, count it, and
 say so when it moved. An unmappable number becomes a literal `?`, which
 is the point - it cannot be mistaken for a line number, so the next
 reader and the next check-citations run both trip over it.)
To 'emit citation' with a number called 'the old line'.
    Set 'citations checked' to 'citations checked' add 1.
    a number called 'the new line' is 'map old line' of 'the old line'.
    a number called 'the verdict' is 'the map verdict'.
    If 'the verdict' is 0 then,
        append "{'the old line'}" to 'the rewritten line',
        If 'listing everything' then, 'report citation' of 'the old line' and "{'the old line'}" and "same"..
    If 'the verdict' is 1 then,
        append "{'the new line'}" to 'the rewritten line',
        Set 'the line changed' to true,
        Set 'citations shifted' to 'citations shifted' add 1,
        'report citation' of 'the old line' and "{'the new line'}" and "shifted".
    If 'the verdict' is 2 then,
        append "{'the new line'}" to 'the rewritten line',
        Set 'the line changed' to true,
        Set 'citations moved' to 'citations moved' add 1,
        'report citation' of 'the old line' and "{'the new line'}" and "moved".
    If 'the verdict' is 3 then,
        append "?" to 'the rewritten line',
        Set 'the line changed' to true,
        Set 'citations broken' to 'citations broken' add 1,
        'report citation' of 'the old line' and "?" and "CHANGED".

(A citation RUN, beginning on a digit: `3143`, `3160-3162`, `3155, 3169`,
 `4451, 4454-4456`. Separators and dashes are copied through byte for
 byte and only the numbers are replaced. The run ends at the first thing
 that is not another number, and the separator before it is left alone -
 which is what keeps a citation followed by a row ID intact rather than
 eating its way into the ID's digits.)
To 'rewrite run' with a number called 'the run start'.
    a number called 'the position' is 'the run start'.
    a boolean called 'more items' is true.
    While 'more items',
        a number called 'the first number' is 'number at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the position',
        Set 'the position' to 'the digits end',
        'emit citation' of 'the first number',
        a number called 'the dash length' is 'dash length at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the position',
        a boolean called 'a digit follows the dash' is false,
        If 'the dash length' is greater than 0 then, Set 'a digit follows the dash' to 'digit at' of 'the line bytes' and {'the position' add 'the dash length'}.
        If 'a digit follows the dash' then,
            'copy slice' of 'the position' and {'the position' add 'the dash length' subtract 1},
            Set 'the position' to 'the position' add 'the dash length',
            a number called 'the second number' is 'number at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the position',
            Set 'the position' to 'the digits end',
            'emit citation' of 'the second number'.
        Set 'more items' to false,
        a boolean called 'a comma here' is 'matches at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the position' and ",",
        a number called 'the after comma' is 'the position' add 1,
        a boolean called 'skipping spaces' is 'a comma here',
        While 'skipping spaces',
            a boolean called 'a space here' is 'matches at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the after comma' and " ",
            If 'a space here' then, Set 'the after comma' to 'the after comma' add 1.
            If 'a space here' is false then, Set 'skipping spaces' to false..
        a boolean called 'a digit follows' is false,
        If 'a comma here' then, Set 'a digit follows' to 'digit at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the after comma'.
        If 'a digit follows' then,
            'copy slice' of 'the position' and {'the after comma' subtract 1},
            Set 'the position' to 'the after comma',
            Set 'more items' to true..
    Set 'the run end' to 'the position'.

(Does an explicit citation of the manual begin at this position? Returns
 the position of its first digit, or 0. Three spellings are in the
 corpus: the colon form everywhere, and the ledgers' Source: headers,
 which write the file name then the word line or lines.)
To 'citation prefix at' with a number called 'the position'.
    a boolean called 'the manual named here' is 'matches at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the position' and "LANGUAGE.md".
    If 'the manual named here' is false then, Return a number, 0.
    a number called 'the after name' is 'the position' add 11.
    a boolean called 'a backtick here' is 'matches at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the after name' and "`".
    If 'a backtick here' then, Set 'the after name' to 'the after name' add 1.
    a number called 'the number start' is 0.
    a boolean called 'a colon here' is 'matches at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the after name' and ":".
    If 'a colon here' then, Set 'the number start' to 'the after name' add 1.
    a boolean called 'a lines word' is 'matches at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the after name' and " lines ".
    If 'a lines word' then, Set 'the number start' to 'the after name' add 7.
    a boolean called 'a line word' is 'matches at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the after name' and " line ".
    If 'a line word' and 'the number start' is 0 then, Set 'the number start' to 'the after name' add 6.
    If 'the number start' is 0 then, Return a number, 0.
    a boolean called 'a number follows' is 'digit at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the number start'.
    If 'a number follows' is false then, Return a number, 0.
    Return a number, 'the number start'.

(---------------------------------------------------------------------
 the ledger tables' `line` column

 A ledger row cites the manual in a bare cell - `| BUF-19 | 3251 | ... |`
 - with no file name in front of it, and those cells are 1378 of the
 roughly 1400 citations in this repo. They are found by the table's own
 HEADER rather than by position, so a table of some other shape (and
 every non-ledger table in docs/) is left alone: the citation column is
 the one whose header cell reads `line` or `LANGUAGE.md lines`, and there
 is no other.
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------)

To 'citation column of' with a text called 'the header row'.
    a buffer called 'the header bytes' is 'the header row'.
    a number called 'the header length' is 'the header bytes's size.
    a number called 'the pipes seen' is 0.
    a number called 'the found column' is 0.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 1.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the header length',
        a number called 'the current byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'the header bytes',
        If 'the current byte' is 124 then,
            Set 'the pipes seen' to 'the pipes seen' add 1,
            a boolean called 'a line header' is 'matches at' of 'the header bytes' and {'the scan position' add 1} and " line |",
            a boolean called 'a range header' is 'matches at' of 'the header bytes' and {'the scan position' add 1} and " LANGUAGE.md lines |",
            If 'a line header' and 'the found column' is 0 then, Set 'the found column' to 'the pipes seen'.
            If 'a range header' and 'the found column' is 0 then, Set 'the found column' to 'the pipes seen'..
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    Return a number, 'the found column'.

(Locate the citation cell inside 'the line bytes' and trim its padding,
 so 'the cell start' is the byte a number would begin on. Both bounds are
 0 when this row has no such cell.)
To 'locate citation cell' with a number called 'the column'.
    Set 'the cell start' to 0.
    Set 'the cell stop' to 0.
    a number called 'the row length' is 'the line bytes's size.
    a number called 'the pipes seen' is 0.
    a number called 'the content start' is 0.
    a number called 'the content stop' is 0.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 1.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the row length',
        a number called 'the current byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'the line bytes',
        If 'the current byte' is 124 then,
            Set 'the pipes seen' to 'the pipes seen' add 1,
            If 'the pipes seen' is 'the column' then, Set 'the content start' to 'the scan position' add 1.
            If 'the pipes seen' is {'the column' add 1} and 'the content stop' is 0 then, Set 'the content stop' to 'the scan position' subtract 1..
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    If 'the content start' is 0 then, Return a number, 0.
    If 'the content stop' is 0 then, Return a number, 0.
    a boolean called 'trimming the front' is true.
    While 'trimming the front' and 'the content start' is less than or equal to 'the content stop',
        a boolean called 'a leading space' is 'matches at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the content start' and " ",
        If 'a leading space' then, Set 'the content start' to 'the content start' add 1.
        If 'a leading space' is false then, Set 'trimming the front' to false..
    a boolean called 'trimming the back' is true.
    While 'trimming the back' and 'the content stop' is greater than or equal to 'the content start',
        a boolean called 'a trailing space' is 'matches at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the content stop' and " ",
        If 'a trailing space' then, Set 'the content stop' to 'the content stop' subtract 1.
        If 'a trailing space' is false then, Set 'trimming the back' to false..
    If 'the content start' is greater than 'the content stop' then, Return a number, 0.
    Set 'the cell start' to 'the content start'.
    Set 'the cell stop' to 'the content stop'.
    Return a number, 'the content start'.

(Is the line after this one a table rule? That is what makes the line
 above it a header row, and the header is what names the citation column.)
To 'a rule follows' with a list called 'the file lines' and a number called 'the index' and a number called 'the line count'.
    a number called 'the next index' is 'the index' add 1.
    If 'the next index' is greater than 'the line count' then, Return a boolean, false.
    a text called 'the next line' is element 'the next index' of 'the file lines'.
    a buffer called 'the next bytes' is 'the next line'.
    a boolean called 'a rule' is 'matches at' of 'the next bytes' and 1 and "|---".
    Return a boolean, 'a rule'.

(A `line` cell this tool will not rewrite, but which holds digits, is a
 citation it cannot see. Say so rather than leave it silently stale.
 Pointless - and pure noise - when the manual did not move at all, so
 both checks are off under an identity map.)
To 'review the cell'.
    If 'the map is identity' then, Return a boolean, false.
    If 'the cell start' is 0 then, Return a boolean, false.
    a boolean called 'starts on a digit' is 'digit at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the cell start'.
    If 'starts on a digit' then, Return a boolean, false.
    a boolean called 'digits in the cell' is 'any digit between' of 'the line bytes' and 'the cell start' and 'the cell stop'.
    If 'digits in the cell' is false then, Return a boolean, false.
    Set 'cells for review' to 'cells for review' add 1.
    Print "{'the current label'}  (the line cell holds digits but does not open with a citation)  REVIEW".
    Return a boolean, true.

To 'review after the run' with a number called 'the after run'.
    If 'the map is identity' then, Return a boolean, false.
    If 'the cell start' is 0 then, Return a boolean, false.
    If 'the after run' is greater than 'the cell stop' then, Return a boolean, false.
    a boolean called 'more digits after' is 'any digit between' of 'the line bytes' and 'the after run' and 'the cell stop'.
    If 'more digits after' is false then, Return a boolean, false.
    Set 'cells for review' to 'cells for review' add 1.
    Print "{'the current label'}  (digits follow the citation run in the line cell)  REVIEW".
    Return a boolean, true.

(---------------------------------------------------------------------
 rewriting one line and one file
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------)

(Rewrites 'the line bytes' into 'the rewritten line'. 'the citation
 column' is 0 unless this line is a row of a table that has one.)
To 'rewrite the line' with a number called 'the citation column'.
    clear 'the rewritten line'.
    Set 'the cell start' to 0.
    Set 'the cell stop' to 0.
    a number called 'the line length' is 'the line bytes's size.
    If 'the citation column' is greater than 0 then, 'locate citation cell' of 'the citation column'.
    'review the cell'.
    a number called 'the position' is 1.
    While 'the position' is less than or equal to 'the line length',
        a boolean called 'handled here' is false,
        a boolean called 'at the cell' is false,
        If 'the cell start' is greater than 0 and 'the position' is 'the cell start' then, Set 'at the cell' to 'digit at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the position'.
        If 'at the cell' then,
            'rewrite run' of 'the position',
            'review after the run' of 'the run end',
            Set 'the position' to 'the run end',
            Set 'handled here' to true.
        If 'handled here' is false then,
            a number called 'the number start' is 'citation prefix at' of 'the position',
            If 'the number start' is greater than 0 then,
                'copy slice' of 'the position' and {'the number start' subtract 1},
                'rewrite run' of 'the number start',
                Set 'the position' to 'the run end',
                Set 'handled here' to true..
        If 'handled here' is false then,
            'copy slice' of 'the position' and 'the position',
            Set 'the position' to 'the position' add 1..

To 'process file' with a text called 'the path'.
    a list called 'the file lines' is 'lines of' of 'the path'.
    a boolean called 'a trailing newline' is 'the file ended with a newline'.
    a number called 'the line count' is 'the file lines's length.
    Set 'files scanned' to 'files scanned' add 1.
    a boolean called 'the file changed' is false.
    clear 'the file output'.
    a number called 'the citation column' is 0.
    a number called 'the index' is 1.
    While 'the index' is less than or equal to 'the line count',
        a text called 'the source line' is element 'the index' of 'the file lines',
        'load the line' of 'the source line',
        a boolean called 'a table row' is 'matches at' of 'the line bytes' and 1 and "|",
        If 'a table row' is false then, Set 'the citation column' to 0.
        a boolean called 'a header row' is false,
        If 'a table row' then, Set 'a header row' to 'a rule follows' of 'the file lines' and 'the index' and 'the line count'.
        If 'a header row' then, Set 'the citation column' to 'citation column of' of 'the source line'.
        Set 'the current label' to "{'the path'}:{'the index'}",
        Set 'the line changed' to false,
        'rewrite the line' of 'the citation column',
        If 'the line changed' then, Set 'the file changed' to true.
        (A dry run needs the report, not the file. Accumulating the whole
         rewritten corpus - about 3MB - only to throw it away is most of
         what the run costs, and a dry run is the mode people sit and
         wait for.)
        If 'applying changes' then, append 'the rewritten line' to 'the file output'.
        If 'applying changes' and 'the index' is less than 'the line count' then, append "\n" to 'the file output'.
        Set 'the index' to 'the index' add 1.
    If 'applying changes' and 'a trailing newline' and 'the line count' is greater than 0 then, append "\n" to 'the file output'.
    If 'the file changed' then, Set 'files rewritten' to 'files rewritten' add 1.
    If 'the file changed' and 'applying changes' then, 'write the output to' of 'the path'.

(---------------------------------------------------------------------
 resolving the two manuals
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------)

(A manual reference - a path if it names one, otherwise a commit in the
 vox repo - to a readable file. Returns the path to read, or "".)
To 'manual file for' with a text called 'the reference' and a text called 'the destination'.
    If 'the reference' is "" then, Return a text, "".
    a boolean called 'a readable path' is 'path exists' of 'the reference'.
    If 'a readable path' then, Return a text, 'the reference'.
    If 'the vox repo is present' is false then, Return a text, "".
    a text called 'the blob' is "{'the reference'}:LANGUAGE.md".
    a text called 'the show command' is "git -C {'shell quote' of 'the vox repo'} show {'shell quote' of 'the blob'} > {'shell quote' of 'the destination'} 2>/dev/null".
    a number called 'the show code' is 'run shell' of 'the show command'.
    If 'the show code' is not 0 then, Return a text, "".
    Return a text, 'the destination'.

(Vox defect, found building this tool (2026-08-22): a global `list` or
 `map` that a FUNCTION reads cannot also be reassigned by a top-level
 `Set` - the compiler then reports "Unknown variable" against the
 declaration itself and refuses the program. Minimal repro in
 docs/ledger/probes/repin/vox-global-list-set.vox. `Set` from INSIDE a
 function is fine, which is why the two manuals are loaded here rather
 than in main.)
To 'load the manuals' with a text called 'the old path' and a text called 'the new path'.
    Set 'the old lines' to 'lines of' of 'the old path'.
    Set 'the new lines' to 'lines of' of 'the new path'.

To 'read the pin'.
    a list called 'the pin lines' is 'lines of' of 'the pin path'.
    a number called 'the pin line count' is 'the pin lines's length.
    a number called 'the pin index' is 1.
    While 'the pin index' is less than or equal to 'the pin line count',
        a text called 'the pin line' is element 'the pin index' of 'the pin lines',
        a buffer called 'the pin bytes' is 'the pin line',
        a boolean called 'a commit line' is 'matches at' of 'the pin bytes' and 1 and "commit ",
        a boolean called 'a lines line' is 'matches at' of 'the pin bytes' and 1 and "lines ",
        If 'a commit line' then, Set 'the pinned commit' to 'rest of line from' of 'the pin bytes' and 8.
        If 'a lines line' then, Set 'the pinned line count' to 'number at' of 'the pin bytes' and 7.
        Set 'the pin index' to 'the pin index' add 1.

(---------------------------------------------------------------------
 --pin
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------)

(Replace the old pinned line count wherever it stands alone in one line
 of INDEX.md's header paragraph, and nowhere else. Only the first ten
 lines are eligible, so no claim text below can be touched by accident,
 and a digit on either side disqualifies a match, so the same digits
 inside a longer number survive.)
To 'replace the count in the line' with a number called 'the old count' and a number called 'the new count' and a number called 'the width' and a number called 'the line number'.
    a number called 'the replacements' is 0.
    a number called 'the position' is 1.
    a number called 'the line length' is 'the line bytes's size.
    While 'the position' is less than or equal to 'the line length',
        a boolean called 'the count here' is false,
        If 'the line number' is less than or equal to 10 then, Set 'the count here' to 'matches at' of 'the line bytes' and 'the position' and "{'the old count'}".
        a boolean called 'a digit before' is 'digit at' of 'the line bytes' and {'the position' subtract 1},
        a boolean called 'a digit after' is 'digit at' of 'the line bytes' and {'the position' add 'the width'},
        If 'a digit before' then, Set 'the count here' to false.
        If 'a digit after' then, Set 'the count here' to false.
        If 'the count here' then,
            append "{'the new count'}" to 'the rewritten line',
            Set 'the replacements' to 'the replacements' add 1,
            Set 'the position' to 'the position' add 'the width'.
        If 'the count here' is false then,
            'copy slice' of 'the position' and 'the position',
            Set 'the position' to 'the position' add 1..
    Return a number, 'the replacements'.

To 'repin the index header' with a number called 'the new line count'.
    If 'the pinned line count' is 0 then, Return a boolean, false.
    a list called 'the index lines' is 'lines of' of 'the index path'.
    a boolean called 'the index ended with a newline' is 'the file ended with a newline'.
    a number called 'the old count width' is 'digit width of' of 'the pinned line count'.
    clear 'the file output'.
    a number called 'the index line count' is 'the index lines's length.
    a number called 'the replacements' is 0.
    a number called 'the index position' is 1.
    While 'the index position' is less than or equal to 'the index line count',
        a text called 'the index line' is element 'the index position' of 'the index lines',
        'load the line' of 'the index line',
        clear 'the rewritten line',
        a number called 'the made' is 'replace the count in the line' of 'the pinned line count' and 'the new line count' and 'the old count width' and 'the index position',
        Set 'the replacements' to 'the replacements' add 'the made',
        append 'the rewritten line' to 'the file output',
        If 'the index position' is less than 'the index line count' then, append "\n" to 'the file output'.
        Set 'the index position' to 'the index position' add 1.
    If 'the index ended with a newline' then, append "\n" to 'the file output'.
    If 'the replacements' is 0 then, Print "INDEX.md: no standalone {'the pinned line count'} in the first ten lines - update the pinned-manual sentence by hand".
    If 'the replacements' is 0 then, Return a boolean, false.
    'write the output to' of 'the index path'.
    Print "INDEX.md: {'the replacements'} header line count(s) updated to {'the new line count'}".
    Return a boolean, true.

To 'rewrite the pin file' with a text called 'the new commit' and a number called 'the new line count' and a text called 'the pin date'.
    a list called 'the pin lines' is 'lines of' of 'the pin path'.
    clear 'the file output'.
    a number called 'the pin line count' is 'the pin lines's length.
    a number called 'the pin index' is 1.
    While 'the pin index' is less than or equal to 'the pin line count',
        a text called 'the pin line' is element 'the pin index' of 'the pin lines',
        a buffer called 'the pin bytes' is 'the pin line',
        a boolean called 'a commit line' is 'matches at' of 'the pin bytes' and 1 and "commit ",
        a boolean called 'a lines line' is 'matches at' of 'the pin bytes' and 1 and "lines ",
        a boolean called 'a pinned line' is 'matches at' of 'the pin bytes' and 1 and "pinned ",
        If 'a commit line' then, append "commit {'the new commit'}" to 'the file output'.
        If 'a lines line' then, append "lines {'the new line count'}" to 'the file output'.
        If 'a pinned line' then, append "pinned {'the pin date'}" to 'the file output'.
        If 'a commit line' is false and 'a lines line' is false and 'a pinned line' is false then, 'append text to the output' of 'the pin line'.
        If 'the pin index' is less than 'the pin line count' then, append "\n" to 'the file output'.
        Set 'the pin index' to 'the pin index' add 1.
    append "\n" to 'the file output'.
    'write the output to' of 'the pin path'.

To 'do the pin'.
    a text called 'the new commit' is "unknown".
    a text called 'the scratch answer' is "{'the scratch directory'}/answer.txt".
    a text called 'the reference' is 'the to argument'.
    a boolean called 'the target is the working tree' is false.
    If 'the reference' is "{'the vox repo'}/LANGUAGE.md" then, Set 'the target is the working tree' to true, Set 'the reference' to "HEAD".
    (Pinning to HEAD while the working tree's LANGUAGE.md differs from it
     would record a commit whose manual is NOT the one just mapped, and
     every later run would then diff from the wrong base.)
    a number called 'the dirty code' is 0.
    If 'the vox repo is present' and 'the target is the working tree' then, Set 'the dirty code' to 'run shell' of "git -C {'shell quote' of 'the vox repo'} diff --quiet HEAD -- LANGUAGE.md".
    If 'the dirty code' is not 0 then,
        Print "repin-citations: {'the vox repo'}/LANGUAGE.md differs from HEAD - refusing to pin to a commit that is not what was mapped",
        Exit 2.
    a number called 'the rev code' is 1.
    If 'the vox repo is present' then, Set 'the rev code' to 'run shell' of "git -C {'shell quote' of 'the vox repo'} rev-parse --short=7 {'shell quote' of 'the reference'} > {'shell quote' of 'the scratch answer'} 2>/dev/null".
    If 'the rev code' is 0 then, Set 'the new commit' to 'first line of' of 'the scratch answer'.
    If 'the new commit' is "" then, Set 'the new commit' to "unknown".
    a number called 'the copy code' is 'run shell' of "cp {'shell quote' of 'the new manual path'} {'shell quote' of 'the pinned copy path'}".
    If 'the copy code' is not 0 then,
        Print "repin-citations: could not refresh {'the pinned copy path'}",
        Exit 2.
    a number called 'the date code' is 'run shell' of "date +%Y-%m-%d > {'shell quote' of 'the scratch answer'}".
    a text called 'the pin date' is 'first line of' of 'the scratch answer'.
    a number called 'the new line count' is 'the new lines's length.
    'rewrite the pin file' of 'the new commit' and 'the new line count' and 'the pin date'.
    Print "pinned: commit {'the new commit'}, {'the new line count'} lines, {'the pinned copy path'} refreshed".
    a boolean called 'the header repinned' is 'repin the index header' of 'the new line count'.

(Vox has no getpid, so the scratch directory's name is read the way
 src/sandbox.vox reads it: the first field of /proc/self/stat. ./vf_* is
 gitignored and already swept by test.sh, which is why every scratch path
 in this repo lives there rather than in /tmp.)
To 'self pid'.
    open a file for reading called 'the stat file' at "/proc/self/stat".
    On error Return a number, 0.
    a buffer called 'the stat bytes'.
    Read from 'the stat file' into 'the stat bytes'.
    Close 'the stat file'.
    a number called 'the pid value' is 'number at' of 'the stat bytes' and 1.
    Return a number, 'the pid value'.

(---------------------------------------------------------------------
 main

 Every multi-line block below is followed by a blank line on purpose: a
 paragraph break force-closes every open clause (LANGUAGE.md, "The
 termination rule", rule 2), which is what keeps the next statement at
 the top level instead of quietly inside the previous `If`.
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------)

If the environment variable "VOX_REPO" exists then, Set 'the vox repo' to environment's "VOX_REPO".

a list called 'the arguments' is arguments's all.
a number called 'the argument count' is 'the arguments's length.
a number called 'the argument index' is 1.
While 'the argument index' is less than or equal to 'the argument count',
    a text called 'the argument' is element 'the argument index' of 'the arguments',
    a boolean called 'takes a value' is false,
    If 'the argument' is "--to" then, Set 'takes a value' to true.
    If 'the argument' is "--from" then, Set 'takes a value' to true.
    If 'the argument' is "--scan" then, Set 'takes a value' to true.
    If 'the argument' is "--vox-repo" then, Set 'takes a value' to true.
    a boolean called 'a value is missing' is false,
    If 'takes a value' and 'the argument index' is 'the argument count' then, Set 'a value is missing' to true.
    If 'a value is missing' then, Print "repin-citations: {'the argument'} needs a value", Exit 2.
    a text called 'the value' is "",
    If 'takes a value' then, Set 'the value' to element {'the argument index' add 1} of 'the arguments', Set 'the argument index' to 'the argument index' add 1.
    If 'the argument' is "--to" then, Set 'the to argument' to 'the value'.
    If 'the argument' is "--from" then, Set 'the from argument' to 'the value'.
    If 'the argument' is "--scan" then, Set 'the scan directory' to 'the value'.
    If 'the argument' is "--vox-repo" then, Set 'the vox repo' to 'the value'.
    If 'the argument' is "--apply" then, Set 'applying changes' to true, Set 'takes a value' to true.
    If 'the argument' is "--dry-run" then, Set 'applying changes' to false, Set 'takes a value' to true.
    If 'the argument' is "--pin" then, Set 'pinning' to true, Set 'takes a value' to true.
    If 'the argument' is "--verbose" then, Set 'listing everything' to true, Set 'takes a value' to true.
    If 'takes a value' is false then, Print "repin-citations: unknown argument {'the argument'}", Exit 2.
    Set 'the argument index' to 'the argument index' add 1.

If 'pinning' and 'applying changes' is false then,
    Print "repin-citations: --pin needs --apply (a pin recorded before the rewrite would double-shift the next run)",
    Exit 2.

a number called 'the pid' is 'self pid'.
Set 'the scratch directory' to "./vf_repin_{'the pid'}".
a number called 'the scratch made' is 'run shell' of "mkdir -p {'shell quote' of 'the scratch directory'}".
If 'the scratch made' is not 0 then,
    Print "repin-citations: cannot create {'the scratch directory'}",
    Exit 2.

Set 'the vox repo is present' to 'path exists' of "{'the vox repo'}/.git".

(the pinned side)
a text called 'the old manual path' is "".
a text called 'the old source' is "".
If 'the from argument' is not "" then,
    Set 'the old source' to 'the from argument',
    Set 'the old manual path' to 'manual file for' of 'the from argument' and "{'the scratch directory'}/old-LANGUAGE.md".

If 'the from argument' is "" then,
    'read the pin',
    Set 'the old source' to "pinned {'the pinned commit'}",
    Set 'the old manual path' to 'manual file for' of 'the pinned commit' and "{'the scratch directory'}/old-LANGUAGE.md".

If 'the from argument' is "" and 'the old manual path' is "" then,
    Set 'the old manual path' to 'the pinned copy path',
    Set 'the old source' to "pinned copy {'the pinned copy path'}".

If 'the old manual path' is "" then,
    Print "repin-citations: cannot read the pinned manual ({'the old source'})",
    Exit 2.

(the target side)
If 'the to argument' is "" then, Set 'the to argument' to "{'the vox repo'}/LANGUAGE.md".
a text called 'the new source' is 'the to argument'.
Set 'the new manual path' to 'manual file for' of 'the to argument' and "{'the scratch directory'}/new-LANGUAGE.md".
If 'the new manual path' is "" then,
    Print "repin-citations: cannot read the target manual ({'the new source'})",
    Exit 2.

'load the manuals' of 'the old manual path' and 'the new manual path'.

(A pin that does not describe the manual it names is a pin nobody can
 trust, and mapping from the wrong base is worse than not mapping.)
a boolean called 'the pin disagrees' is false.
If 'the from argument' is "" and 'the pinned line count' is greater than 0 and 'the old lines's length is not 'the pinned line count' then, Set 'the pin disagrees' to true.
If 'the pin disagrees' then,
    Print "repin-citations: the pinned manual is {'the old lines's length} lines but PINNED-MANUAL says {'the pinned line count'} - refusing to map from it",
    Exit 2.

a text called 'the diff path' is "{'the scratch directory'}/manual.diff".
a number called 'the diff code' is 'run shell' of "diff -U0 {'shell quote' of 'the old manual path'} {'shell quote' of 'the new manual path'} > {'shell quote' of 'the diff path'}".
If 'the diff code' is greater than 1 then,
    Print "repin-citations: diff failed ({'the diff code'})",
    Exit 2.

'read hunks from' of 'the diff path'.
Set 'the map is identity' to false.
If 'hunk old start's length is 0 then, Set 'the map is identity' to true.

Print "repin-citations: {'the old source'} ({'the old lines's length} lines) -> {'the new source'} ({'the new lines's length} lines)".
Print "hunks: {'hunk old start's length}".
If 'the map is identity' then, Print "the manual has not moved - every citation maps to itself, no change".

(File discovery. The pinned copy of the manual is deliberately excluded:
 it is the reference, not a citer, and rewriting it would destroy the very
 thing the next run measures from.)
a text called 'the listing path' is "{'the scratch directory'}/files.txt".
a text called 'the find command' is "find docs/ledger -type f \\( -name '*.md' -o -name '*.vox' \\) -not -path 'docs/ledger/pinned/*' > {'shell quote' of 'the listing path'}; find src scripts tests -maxdepth 1 -type f -name '*.vox' >> {'shell quote' of 'the listing path'}; test -f CLAUDE.md && echo CLAUDE.md >> {'shell quote' of 'the listing path'}; sort -o {'shell quote' of 'the listing path'} {'shell quote' of 'the listing path'}".
If 'the scan directory' is not "" then, Set 'the find command' to "find {'shell quote' of 'the scan directory'} -type f \\( -name '*.md' -o -name '*.vox' \\) | sort > {'shell quote' of 'the listing path'}".
a number called 'the find code' is 'run shell' of 'the find command'.
a list called 'the files' is 'lines of' of 'the listing path'.
a number called 'the file count' is 'the files's length.
If 'the file count' is 0 then,
    Print "repin-citations: no files to scan",
    Exit 2.

Print "".
Print "file:line  old -> new  status".
a number called 'the file index' is 1.
While 'the file index' is less than or equal to 'the file count',
    a text called 'one file' is element 'the file index' of 'the files',
    'process file' of 'one file',
    Set 'the file index' to 'the file index' add 1.

Print "".
Print "citations: {'citations checked'} checked, {'citations shifted'} shifted, {'citations moved'} moved, {'citations broken'} CHANGED, {'cells for review'} for review".
Print "files: {'files scanned'} scanned, {'files rewritten'} changed".
If 'applying changes' is false and 'files rewritten' is greater than 0 then, Print "dry run - nothing written; re-run with --apply".
If 'pinning' then, 'do the pin'.

a number called 'the sweep code' is 'run shell' of "rm -rf {'shell quote' of 'the scratch directory'}".

If 'citations broken' is greater than 0 then,
    Print "",
    Print "{'citations broken'} citation(s) name a line whose text CHANGED and could not be found again; each is now `?`. Read the manual and the row together, and write the line the claim actually lives on now.",
    Exit 1.
